s/link-foundation/browser-commander/zipball/v0.17.0","body":"### Minor Changes\n\n- Restore the release pipeline. `const { $ } = await use('command-stream')` returned `undefined` on the Node 24 the release jobs pin, because Node 23 added a synthetic `'module.exports'` named export for CommonJS namespaces that use-m's unwrapping does not recognise, so every JS and Rust release died on its first shell call with `TypeError: $ is not a function`. Dependencies now load through `scripts/use-module.mjs`, which detects that namespace shape and unwraps it. Manifest fields are read by TOML table through `scripts/read-manifest.mjs` instead of a line-anchored `grep`, which matched duplicate keys in other tables and wrote two lines into `$GITHUB_OUTPUT`. The duplication gate had `format` set to a reporter name, so jscpd scanned zero files and passed unconditionally; it now scans the tree against a recorded baseline.\n  \n  Add the `fingerprint` subsystem, which removes the differences between a browser this library controls and one started by hand. `navigator.webdriver` stays false because the `AutomationControlled` Blink feature is disabled at launch, and `launchBrowser()` no longer passes the engine defaults a real Chrome does not carry. `resolveFingerprintProfile()` validates the 19 environment fields a page can read -- user agent and client hints, languages and locale, time zone, platform, cores, memory, screen, viewport, touch, WebGL strings, geolocation and the media preferences -- `createFingerprintPreset()` builds internally consistent Windows, macOS, Linux and Android machines, and `applyFingerprint()` installs a profile over CDP for both Playwright and Puppeteer. `FINGERPRINT_LIMITATIONS` documents what still cannot be made identical, and `relevantFingerprintLimitations()` narrows it to the entries a given profile and browser actually hit. The excluded defaults include Playwright's unconditional `--enable-unsafe-swiftshader`, which would otherwise give an automated browser a SwiftShader WebGL context on a machine where a hand-started Chrome has none."}ℹ️ Found Minor Changes section
Release	Format GitHub release notes	2026-09-05T15:22:26.1600569Z ℹ️ Looking up PR for commit 4f7af544a15eb89d00d214294dc2a3ad08882a4e (from workflow)
Release	Format GitHub release notes	2026-09-05T15:22:26.5994604Z [{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-foundation/browser-commander/pulls/82","id":4449836715,"node_id":"PR_kwDOQYTTi88AAAABCTseqw","html_url":"https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/pull/82","diff_url":"https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/pull/82.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/pull/82.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-foundation/browser-commander/issues/82","number":82,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"Fix every false positive, false negative, warning and error in CI/CD","user":{"login":"konard","id":1431904,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard","html_url":"https://github.com/konard","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixes #81.\n\nThe issue asked for every false positive, false negative, warning and error in\nthis repository's CI to be found and fixed, for the three\n`*-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template` repositories to be used as the\nreference, and for anything that turned out to be a template defect to be\nreported there too. All of that is in this one pull request.\n\nAll nine workflows are green on this branch, and — this is the part that took\nthe work — they are green because they check things, not because they stopped\nchecking.\n\n## What was actually broken\n\nThe three failing runs the issue lists had three distinct causes, and finding\nthem turned up sixteen more. The full write-up is\n[`dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/analysis/root-causes.md`](dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/analysis/root-causes.md)\n(RC-1 … RC-19); the short version:\n\n**Errors — the three red runs**\n\n- **RC-1 / RC-3 — a structured file read by line shape.** `python/` scraped its\n  version with `grep -Po '(?<=^version = \")[^\"]*'`. TOML is table-structured, so\n  that matched `version` in *every* table and wrote two lines into\n  `$GITHUB_OUTPUT`: `Invalid format 'literal: pyproject.toml: project.version'`.\n  `head -1` is not a fix — it makes the answer depend on table order. The write\n  path had the mirror-image bug in an unscoped `re.sub`. Replaced with\n  `scripts/read-manifest.mjs` and `python/scripts/read_manifest.py`, which\n  address fields by table path through a real parser, and a workflow policy rule\n  that now rejects any manifest field scraped with `grep`/`sed`/`awk`.\n- **RC-2 — an unpinned dependency changed its module system under us.** The JS\n  and Rust release jobs pin `node-version: '24.x'` and died on\n  `TypeError: $ is not a function`. Node 23 added a synthetic `'module.exports'`\n  named export to CommonJS namespaces; `use-m` does not list it as metadata, so\n  its unwrap is skipped and the real exports stay one level down. Measured:\n  Node 20 and 22 → 32 keys, works; Node 24 → 2 keys, broken.\n  `scripts/use-module.mjs` recognises the shape and unwraps it, and a test fails\n  if a new script goes back to calling `use(...)` directly. Upstream this is\n  [link-foundation/use-m#72](https://github.com/link-foundation/use-m/issues/72),\n  already open with the right fix — so rather than restate it we\n  [corrected its version boundary](https://github.com/link-foundation/use-m/issues/72#issuecomment-5552037466):\n  it says Node ≥ 22.12, but v22.23.2 is still fine and v23.11.1 is where the\n  synthetic key appears, so the regression test it proposes would pass and still\n  miss this.\n\n**False negatives — checks that passed without checking**\n\n- The duplication gate ran against a path that held no source (RC-4).\n- The workflows themselves were never linted — `actionlint` and `zizmor` found\n  SC2086 and 39 injection/permission findings once they were pointed at them\n  (RC-5, RC-6, RC-8).\n- A `|| true` masked a real Prettier failure (RC-7).\n- **RC-14 — husky could never see `.git`.** husky 9.1.7 *returns* its errors as\n  text and exits 0, so `\"prepare\": \"husky || true\"` reported a successful\n  install of no hooks at all, with `core.hooksPath` unset. Nothing ran locally.\n- **RC-15 — the linter could not see half the repository's JavaScript.** ESLint\n  was configured inside `js/`, so `scripts/` and `experiments/` were unlinted. A\n  root config covers them now, and the 10 warnings that surfaced are cleared\n  rather than excluded.\n- **RC-19 — mypy could not see the CI scripts either.** RC-15 is ESLint\n  configured inside `js/`; `python/` had the identical shape and survived the\n  first pass, because the workflow runs `mypy src` and `python/scripts/` — which\n  this pull request itself added to — was never type-checked. It cannot be\n  folded into the existing step: `pyproject.toml` targets `python_version =\n  \"3.9\"` to match the package's `requires-python`, and under that target\n  `import tomllib` is correctly an error. The helpers run on the job's\n  interpreter, not the package's floor, so they get their own invocation and\n  their own target.\n- **RC-16 — a job killed by `timeout-minutes` is reported `cancelled`, not\n  `failed`,** and a run whose only casualty is cancelled has conclusion\n  `cancelled`. Nothing turned that red. `scripts/check-pipeline-status.sh` is a\n  terminal gate now, wired into **all nine** workflows.\n\n**Errors — two this pull request introduced, found by CodeQL on itself**\n\n- **RC-18 — a pattern built from an argument, and a bump that could not fail.**\n  The new manifest readers interpolated the caller's field name into\n  `new RegExp()`. CodeQL labelled it `js/regex-injection`, which understates it:\n  it is a correctness bug in ordinary use, because looking up `a.b` silently\n  reads `axb` and looking up `a(` throws `SyntaxError` instead of reporting a\n  missing field. Both were reproduced before the fix. Field names are compared\n  literally now. Fixing it exposed a third defect in the same code, present in\n  *both* language implementations: the write path used `String.replace`, which\n  returns the string unchanged when nothing matches — so a version bump that\n  wrote nothing produced no error, no diff, and a release publishing the old\n  version, exactly the silence of a `|| true`. It throws now, and the rewrite\n  splices by position so spacing and trailing comments survive.\n\n**False positives — checks that failed for non-defects**\n\n- Three checks that this repository cannot satisfy, failing on that rather than\n  on a defect (RC-11, RC-12).\n- **RC-9 — the audit's own false positive.** `python -m venv` seeds pip into the\n  environment being audited, so `pip-audit --path` reports pip's advisories as\n  the project's.\n- **RC-13 — the link checker could not tell a redirect from a corpse**, and\n  failed on healthy links.\n- **RC-17 — the supersede.** Fixing RC-16 naively creates the opposite bug:\n  `cancel-in-progress: true` means a rapid second push to `main` cancels the\n  first run *correctly*. The gate asks `git ls-remote` whether the commit under\n  test is still the branch head, which is the only evidence that separates an\n  overrun from a supersede, and is why no `needs`-only action can decide it.\n\n**Warnings**\n\n0 ESLint errors and 0 warnings across `js/`, `scripts/` and `experiments/`. The\nCI side was checked through the check-run annotations API rather than by\ngrepping logs, because a job can emit `::warning` without the word appearing in\na readable line. Across all nine runs the only annotations left are two\n`notice`s and one deliberate warning (RC-11).\n\n## Reproductions and tests\n\nEvery fix has a test, and each reproduction was run before the fix was written.\n16 new test files; the reproductions are kept in\n[`experiments/ci-repro/`](experiments/ci-repro/) so a future regression can be\ndemonstrated rather than argued about — including\n`check-jscpd-new-clone-reporting.mjs`, which exists because it **disproved** a\nclaim this PR was about to file upstream.\n\n## Debug output, default off\n\n`scripts/debug-print.mjs` is an opt-in logger (`CI_DEBUG=1`) used by the\npipeline scripts, so the next interop change is diagnosable from the CI log\ninstead of a bare `TypeError`. `scripts/run-with-budget-warning.sh` warns at 70%\nof a step's budget while the overrun can still be acted on, and\n`docs/CI-TIMEOUT-BUDGETS.md` plus `ci-timeout-budgets.test.js` keep every budget\nunder 70% of its job's `timeout-minutes`.\n\n## Templates: adopted, and reported back\n\nThe full file-tree comparison against the three templates is\n[`dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/templates/file-tree-diff.md`](dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/templates/file-tree-diff.md).\nAdopted what was missing here (pre-commit, secretlint, `audit_dependencies.py`,\nfile-line limits, version-check, and more), and `scripts/check-ci-workflows.mjs`\nencodes the adopted rules as a policy check so the comparison cannot go stale.\n\nSeven defects found *in the templates* were reported there, each with a\nreproduction that was run and a suggested fix that was executed before it was\nproposed — bodies kept in\n[`dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/templates/upstream-reports/`](dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/templates/upstream-reports/):\n\n| Issue | Defect |\n| --- | --- |\n| [python#67](https://github.com/link-foundation/python-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/67), [rust#155](https://github.com/link-foundation/rust-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/155) | the same table-blind manifest read as RC-1, in both templates |\n| [python#68](https://github.com/link-foundation/python-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/68) | `audit_dependencies.py` audits pip, and `check=True` discards the advisory table |\n| [js#166](https://github.com/link-foundation/js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/166) | husky exits 0 for every failure it has |\n| [js#167](https://github.com/link-foundation/js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/167), [python#69](https://github.com/link-foundation/python-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/69), [rust#156](https://github.com/link-foundation/rust-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/156) | the status gate covers one workflow, and cannot tell an overrun from a supersede |\n\nTwo further drafts were **withdrawn** rather than filed, which is the same\ndiscipline the issue is asking for pointed at our own output: the jscpd finding\nis already js#157, and the \"hidden zizmor findings\" turned out not to be defects\n(python's are a step output whose only values are the literals `.` and `python`;\nrust's are release-writer checkouts that must hold the token because they push).\nFiling those would have added exactly the noise this issue exists to remove.\n\n## Evidence\n\nEverything the analysis cites is committed under\n[`dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/`](dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/): the raw CI logs,\nthe issue and PR snapshots, the template comparison, the research, and four\nanalysis documents —\n[`timeline.md`](dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/analysis/timeline.md),\n[`requirements.md`](dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/analysis/requirements.md) (every\nrequirement R-1 … R-22 with its status),\n[`root-causes.md`](dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/analysis/root-causes.md) and\n[`existing-solutions.md`](dev/log/issues/81/pulls/82/analysis/existing-solutions.md),\nwhich records for each root cause what off-the-shelf component solves it,\nwhether it was adopted, and — when it was not — the repository-specific fact the\ngeneric component cannot know.\n\n","created_at":"2026-09-05T09:20:08Z","updated_at":"2026-09-05T15:19:29Z","closed_at":"2026-09-05T15:19:29Z","merged_at":"2026-09-05T15:19:29Z","merge_commit_sha":"4f7af544a15eb89d00d214294dc2a3ad08882a4e","assignees":[{"login":"konard","id":1431904,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard","html_url":"https://github.com/konard","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"labels":[],"milestone":null,"draft":false,"commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-foundation/browser-commander/pulls/82/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-foundation/browser-commander/pulls/82/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-foundation/browser-commander/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-foundation/browser-commander/issues/82/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-foundation/browser-commander/statuses/c216703662b60915c91f99f0e1ef2a4b727e1839","head":{"label":"link-foundation:issue-81-9d4c346eb143","r